### Advertising Proxy
- **Presenter:** Ted Lemon
- **Slide Deck:** [Advertising Proxy](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-dnssd-advertising-proxy-01)
- **Draft:** `draft-ietf-dnssd-advertising-proxy`

#### Technical Content
- Updated document following progress on `draft-ietf-dnssd-tsr`.
- **Address Selection Rules:**
  - *Link-Local Addresses:* Only advertised if confirmed to originate from the local link.
  - *Global Addresses (GUA/ULA):* Select valid source/destination pairs matching the routing table; omit addresses that cannot form valid pairs unless routing table accuracy is uncertain (in which case, fall back to advertising all available addresses).
- **Internal Consistency:** Requires advertising proxies to resolve hanging pointers if a dependent record (e.g., service instance) references a name that encountered a conflict.
- **Deployment Contexts:**
  - *Managed Zone:* Managed infrastructure domain.
  - *Infrastructure Zone:* CE router controlling local resolver.
  - *Ad-Hoc Zone:* SNAC router without infrastructure DNS control.
  - In managed and infrastructure topologies, conflicts can be resolved by redirecting service resolution directly to unicast DNS rather than mDNS.
- **API Browsing Compatibility:** Stuart Cheshire noted that standard browsing APIs (e.g., macOS) store (name, type, domain) triplets; as long as clients retain the full triplet, resolving services redirected to unicast domains functions correctly.
- **mDNS Address Record TTL:** Discussed increasing address record TTLs from 120 seconds (RFC 6762 default) to 75 minutes in the proxy context to prevent frequent multicast refresh traffic. Stuart Cheshire and Lorenzo Colitti supported this change for proxies, noting Happy Eyeballs mitigates risks associated with stale address records.

